      {
        id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1', name: 'Biostatistics & Epidemiological Principles', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', legacyId: 'USMLE_11_2', topics: [
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1', name: 'Epidemiological Study Designs: Observational & Experimental (RCT)', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_003', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_001'], aliases: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001', 'USMLE_11_2_001', 'Epidemiological study designs framework and hierarchy:', 'Cross-Sectional Study prevalence study observational assessment of exposure and disease outcome simultaneously at a single point in time snapshot cannot establish temporal relationship or causality provides disease PREVALENCE e.g. survey of smoking and COPD in community', 'Case-Control Study observational retrospective study identifies group with disease Cases and comparison group without disease Controls looks back in time to compare frequency of past exposure calculates ODDS RATIO OR fast inexpensive ideal for RARE diseases or diseases with long latency prone to Recall Bias and selection bias cannot calculate incidence or relative risk', 'Cohort Study observational study follows defined groups over time categorized by presence Exposed or absence Unexposed of a risk factor followed prospectively or retrospectively to compare disease INCIDENCE calculates RELATIVE RISK RR and Attributable Risk ideal for RARE EXPOSURES expensive long duration prone to Loss to Follow-up attrition bias', 'Randomized Controlled Trial RCT gold standard experimental interventional study participants randomly allocated to treatment or control/placebo groups: Randomization balances both known and unknown confounders Blinding single double or triple eliminates observer and placebo effects Intention-to-Treat ITT analysis analyzes all patients in originally assigned group regardless of adherence or dropouts preserves randomization benefits', 'Crossover Study subjects receive sequence of different treatments e.g. Drug A then Drug B separated by Washout Period subjects serve as their own controls minimizes inter-individual variability', 'Twin and Adoption Studies quantify genetic vs environmental contributions to phenotype: Monozygotic vs Dizygotic concordance rates adoption studies compare adoptees to biological vs adoptive parents'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_002', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1', name: 'Measures of Morbidity & Mortality: Incidence, Prevalence & Duration', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 60, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_004'], aliases: ['Epidemiological measures of disease occurrence:', 'Prevalence proportion of a population with a disease at a specified point in time (Point Prevalence) or over a specified interval (Period Prevalence): Prevalence = Total existing cases / Total population at risk at that time dimensionless fraction or percentage', 'Incidence rate of occurrence of NEW cases of disease arising in a population initially at risk over a specified time interval: Cumulative Incidence = Number of new cases / Population at risk at start of observation period; Incidence Rate (Incidence Density) = Number of new cases / Total person-time at risk', 'Mathematical relationship between Prevalence and Incidence: In steady-state conditions with low prevalence (<10%) Prevalence ≈ Incidence x Average Disease Duration (P = I x D): A new curative treatment or fatal course decreases duration therefore DECREASES prevalence while incidence remains unchanged; A new life-prolonging maintenance treatment (e.g. insulin for type 1 diabetes, HAART for HIV) prolongs survival duration therefore INCREASES prevalence while incidence remains unchanged; An effective primary vaccine decreases incidence therefore DECREASES prevalence', 'Mortality Rates: Crude Mortality Rate = Total deaths / Total population per year; Cause-Specific Mortality Rate = Deaths from specific cause / Total population; Case Fatality Rate = Deaths from specific disease / Total confirmed cases of that disease measure of disease lethality/virulence; Attack Rate = Exposed individuals developing disease / Total exposed individuals (foodborne outbreaks)'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_003', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1', name: 'Measures of Association: Odds Ratio, Relative Risk, Attributable Risk, NNT & NNH', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_004'], aliases: ['Quantification of association using standard 2x2 contingency table (Exposed/Unexposed by Diseased/Non-diseased: a, b, c, d):', 'Odds Ratio OR ratio of odds of exposure in diseased cases (a/c) compared to non-diseased controls (b/d): OR = (a x d) / (b x c) used in Case-Control studies: Rare Disease Assumption: When disease prevalence is very low (<5%) OR closely approximates Relative Risk RR OR = 1 no association, OR > 1 increased risk, OR < 1 protective', 'Relative Risk RR (Risk Ratio) ratio of risk of disease in exposed group [a / (a+b)] compared to risk in unexposed group [c / (c+d)]: RR = [a/(a+b)] / [c/(c+d)] used in Cohort studies and RCTs: RR = 1 null effect, RR > 1 exposure increases disease risk, RR < 1 exposure is protective', 'Relative Risk Reduction RRR proportion of baseline risk eliminated by intervention: RRR = 1 - RR = (Risk unexposed - Risk exposed) / Risk unexposed', 'Absolute Risk Reduction ARR difference in risk between control and exposed/treated group: ARR = Risk unexposed - Risk exposed = [c/(c+d)] - [a/(a+b)] more clinically meaningful than RRR', 'Number Needed to Treat NNT number of patients needing treatment for one additional patient to benefit: NNT = 1 / ARR rounded UP to nearest whole integer', 'Attributable Risk AR (Risk Difference) excess risk of disease in exposed group attributable to exposure: AR = Risk exposed - Risk unexposed = [a/(a+b)] - [c/(c+d)]', 'Number Needed to Harm NNH number of patients exposed to risk factor or drug for one additional person to suffer harm: NNH = 1 / AR rounded DOWN to nearest whole integer'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_004', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1', name: 'Diagnostic Test Metrics: Sensitivity, Specificity, PPV & NPV (Prevalence Shifts)', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_005', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_002'], aliases: ['Diagnostic test performance evaluated against reference Gold Standard (2x2 table: Test +/- by Disease +/-: TP, FP, FN, TN):', 'Sensitivity (True Positive Rate TPR) probability that a diseased person tests positive: Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN) = TP / All Diseased: High sensitivity test has very few false negatives ideal for SCREENING SnNOut when high sensitivity test is negative it rules OUT disease', 'Specificity (True Negative Rate TNR) probability that a non-diseased person tests negative: Specificity = TN / (TN + FP) = TN / All Non-diseased: High specificity test has very few false positives ideal for CONFIRMATION SpPIn when high specificity test is positive it rules IN disease; False Positive Rate FPR = 1 - Specificity; False Negative Rate FNR = 1 - Sensitivity', 'Positive Predictive Value PPV probability that a person with a positive test result truly has the disease: PPV = TP / (TP + FP): PPV is DIRECTLY DEPENDENT on disease PREVALENCE: As disease prevalence increases PPV increases markedly in low prevalence populations false positives outnumber true positives', 'Negative Predictive Value NPV probability that a person with a negative test result is truly disease-free: NPV = TN / (TN + FN): NPV is INVERSELY RELATED to prevalence: As disease prevalence increases NPV decreases in low prevalence populations NPV is exceptionally high', 'Sensitivity and Specificity are intrinsic physical properties of the diagnostic test and do NOT change with disease prevalence PPV and NPV change directly with prevalence'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_005', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1', name: 'ROC Curves, Cutoff Threshold Shifts & Likelihood Ratios', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_004', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001'], aliases: ['Receiver Operating Characteristic ROC curves and threshold adjustments:', 'ROC Curve graphical plot of Sensitivity (True Positive Rate, Y-axis) versus 1 - Specificity (False Positive Rate, X-axis) across all possible diagnostic cutoff thresholds: Area Under Curve AUC represents overall diagnostic accuracy: AUC = 1.0 perfect test 100% sensitivity and 100% specificity; AUC = 0.5 diagonal straight line 45 degrees equivalent to flipping a coin useless test; Curve closest to upper left corner has best overall discrimination', 'Shifting Diagnostic Cutoff Threshold:', 'Lowering cutoff threshold (e.g. lowering blood glucose cutoff for diabetes screening): Catches more diseased cases: Increases Sensitivity (TPR rises) and Increases NPV; but catches more false positives: Decreases Specificity (FPR rises) and Decreases PPV', 'Raising cutoff threshold (e.g. raising troponin cutoff to confirm MI): Fewer false positives: Increases Specificity (FPR drops) and Increases PPV; but misses more diseased cases: Decreases Sensitivity (FNR rises) and Decreases NPV', 'Likelihood Ratios LRs quantify how much a test result changes the odds of having disease INDEPENDENT of prevalence: Likelihood Ratio Positive LR+ = Sensitivity / (1 - Specificity) = TPR / FPR LR+ > 10 strongly confirms/rules in disease; Likelihood Ratio Negative LR- = (1 - Sensitivity) / Specificity = FNR / TNR LR- < 0.1 strongly excludes/rules out disease; Pre-test Odds x Likelihood Ratio = Post-test Odds Fagan nomogram calculation'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_006', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1', name: 'Distributions, Central Tendency & Skewness', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 60, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_007', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_008'], aliases: ['Measures of central tendency and shape of continuous data distributions:', 'Measures of Central Tendency: Mean arithmetic average sensitive to extreme outliers; Median middle value 50th percentile robust resilient to outliers preferred for skewed data; Mode most frequently occurring value', 'Normal (Gaussian) Distribution symmetrical bell-shaped curve where Mean = Median = Mode: Empirical Rule: Mean ± 1 Standard Deviation SD encompasses 68.2% of data; Mean ± 2 SD encompasses 95.4% of data (exact 1.96 SD = 95.0%); Mean ± 3 SD encompasses 99.7% of data', 'Standard Error of the Mean SEM measure of precision of sample mean as estimate of true population mean: SEM = SD / sqrt(n) As sample size n increases SEM decreases sample mean approaches population mean; 95% Confidence Interval CI = Mean ± 1.96 x SEM If 95% CI of a difference includes 0 null hypothesis cannot be rejected (p > 0.05); If 95% CI of RR or OR includes 1.0 null hypothesis cannot be rejected', 'Positive Skew (Right-Skewed distribution) long asymmetric tail pointing towards positive/higher values pulled by high outliers: Mean > Median > Mode (Mean is pulled furthest to the right)', 'Negative Skew (Left-Skewed distribution) long asymmetric tail pointing towards negative/lower values pulled by low outliers: Mean < Median < Mode (Mean is pulled furthest to the left)', 'Bimodal Distribution two distinct peaks indicates two different underlying subpopulations (e.g. Hodgkin lymphoma age distribution peaks at 20-30 and >65)'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'average' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_007', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1', name: 'Hypothesis Testing: Alpha (Type I), Beta (Type II), Statistical Power & P-Values', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_006', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_008'], aliases: ['Principles of inferential hypothesis testing:', 'Null Hypothesis H0 statement of NO difference, no association, or no effect between groups; Alternative Hypothesis H1 (Ha) statement that a real difference or effect exists', 'Type I Error (Alpha error, False Positive): Rejecting null hypothesis H0 when H0 is actually TRUE (declaring a difference exists when there is none) Alpha level typically preset at 0.05 (5% chance of falsely claiming a significant difference)', 'Type II Error (Beta error, False Negative): Failing to reject null hypothesis H0 when H0 is actually FALSE (missing a true difference or effect) Beta typically set at 0.10 to 0.20 (10-20%)', 'Statistical Power = 1 - Beta probability of correctly rejecting the null hypothesis when H0 is false (probability of detecting a true difference when one genuinely exists): Power is increased by: 1 Increasing Sample Size n (most common method), 2 Increasing Effect Size or difference between groups, 3 Increasing Alpha significance threshold (e.g. from 0.01 to 0.05), 4 Decreasing measurement variability / standard deviation', 'P-value probability of obtaining a test statistic as extreme as or more extreme than observed value purely by random chance assuming null hypothesis is true: If p < alpha (p < 0.05) reject null hypothesis statistically significant; If p >= alpha fail to reject null hypothesis not statistically significant'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_008', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1', name: 'Inferential Statistical Tests: t-Test, ANOVA, Chi-Square, Fisher Exact & Correlation (r)', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_006', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_007'], aliases: ['Selecting appropriate statistical hypothesis test based on variable types and study design:', 'Two-Sample Independent t-Test (Student t-test) compares the MEANS of a single continuous quantitative variable between TWO independent categorical groups (e.g. comparing mean systolic blood pressure in treatment drug vs placebo group)', 'Paired t-Test compares the MEANS of a continuous variable in TWO dependent or matched groups (e.g. comparing blood pressure BEFORE and AFTER intervention in the same individuals)', 'ANOVA Analysis of Variance compares the MEANS of a single continuous variable across THREE or more categorical groups (e.g. comparing mean HbA1c among patients on metformin vs sulfonylurea vs insulin)', 'Chi-Square Test (χ²) compares categorical frequencies or proportions between TWO or more categorical variables arranged in a contingency table (e.g. comparing proportion of smokers vs non-smokers who develop lung cancer Yes/No in 2x2 table)', 'Fisher Exact Test non-parametric alternative to Chi-Square used for 2x2 categorical tables when sample size is small or expected cell counts are less than 5', 'Pearson Correlation Coefficient (r) measures the strength and direction of a LINEAR relationship between TWO CONTINUOUS variables ranges from -1.0 (perfect negative linear correlation) through 0.0 (no linear relationship) to +1.0 (perfect positive linear correlation); Coefficient of Determination (r²) represents the proportion of variance in dependent variable explained by independent variable (e.g. r = 0.8 means r² = 0.64 or 64% of variance explained)'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' }
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2', name: 'Epidemiological Bias, Confounding & Study Methodology', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', legacyId: 'USMLE_11_Bias', topics: [
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_001', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2', name: 'Selection Bias, Berkson Bias, Healthy Worker Effect & Attrition Bias', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_002'], aliases: ['Selection bias non-random assignment or non-representative sampling leading to distorted exposure-outcome association:', 'Berkson Bias (Admission Rate Bias) selection bias occurring when hospital inpatients are used as controls; hospitalized patients have higher rates of comorbidities multiple illnesses and smoking/alcohol exposures than the general community population artificially skewing odds ratios', 'Healthy Worker Effect occupational selection bias occurring when studying working populations compared to general population; employed individuals are systematically healthier than general population which includes chronically ill disabled and elderly people underestimates occupational health risks', 'Non-Response Bias occurs when individuals who choose to participate in a study or survey differ systematically in demographics behaviors or health status from those who decline to participate', 'Loss to Follow-Up Bias (Attrition Bias) in prospective cohort studies when differential dropout rates occur between exposed and unexposed or diseased and non-diseased individuals biasing relative risk estimates', 'Susceptibility Bias (Confounding by Indication) when a treatment is selectively prescribed to sicker patients making the drug appear to cause worse outcomes', 'Minimization Strategies: Random probability sampling, high follow-up/retention rates >80%, incident case selection rather than prevalent cases'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_002', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2', name: 'Information Bias: Recall, Observer-Expectancy, Hawthorne & Social Desirability', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 60, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001'], aliases: ['Information bias (Observation / Misclassification Bias) systematic error in measurement or collection of exposure or outcome data:', 'Recall Bias classic in retrospective case-control studies where subjects with disease (cases) remember and report past exposures more accurately or with exaggeration compared to healthy controls (e.g. mother of a child born with congenital malformation remembers mild infections or medications during pregnancy far better than mother of healthy infant) minimized by using medical records or blinded questionnaires', 'Observer-Expectancy Bias (Pygmalion Effect / Experimenter Bias) investigator knowledge of treatment allocation or expectation subconsciously influences outcome measurement interpretation or patient management prevented by Double-Blinding', 'Hawthorne Effect (Observer Effect) study participants alter their natural behavior lifestyle adherence or performance simply because they know they are being observed or monitored', 'Social Desirability Bias participants systematically underreport stigmatized behaviors (alcohol intake, illicit drugs, sexual partners) and overreport healthy behaviors (exercise, diet, medication adherence) prevented by anonymous self-administered surveys', 'Verification Bias (Workup Bias) diagnostic test verification bias where patients with positive screening results are preferentially referred for definitive gold-standard biopsy while negative screening patients are not artificially inflates sensitivity', 'Misclassification: Differential misclassification errors differ between groups biases towards or away from null; Non-differential misclassification random measurement error across all groups always biases TOWARDS the null hypothesis (dilutes association)'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_003', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2', name: 'Confounding vs Effect Modification (Interaction) & Stratified Analysis', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_003'], aliases: ['Distinguishing confounding from effect modification critical USMLE concept:', 'Confounding occurs when an extraneous third variable is causally associated with the exposure AND independently causally associated with the outcome, but is NOT an intermediate step on the causal pathway (e.g. smoking is a confounder in apparent association between coffee drinking and pancreatic cancer; coffee drinkers smoke more, and smoking causes cancer)', 'Effect Modification (Interaction) occurs when a third variable modifies the biological effect of the exposure on the outcome across different subgroups; the exposure produces different magnitude or direction of effect in different strata (e.g. smoking increases DVT risk dramatically in women taking oral contraceptives, but not in women not on OCPs; statins cause myopathy more frequently in patients with SLCO1B1 gene variants)', 'Method to Distinguish: STRATIFIED ANALYSIS: Perform analysis separately within each category of the third variable: If the crude relative risk differs from stratum-specific estimates, but the stratum-specific relative risks are EQUAL to each other, it is CONFOUNDING (association disappears after controlling); If the stratum-specific relative risks are SIGNIFICANTLY DIFFERENT from each other, it is EFFECT MODIFICATION (biological interaction)', 'Controlling Confounding: In study design: 1 Randomization (controls known and unknown confounders), 2 Matching cases and controls, 3 Restriction of eligibility criteria; In data analysis: 1 Stratified analysis (Mantel-Haenszel), 2 Multivariable regression modeling; Effect modification is NOT a bias cannot be controlled and must be reported'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_004', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2', name: 'Screening Test Biases: Lead-Time Bias & Length-Time Bias', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 60, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PATH_4_008', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_004'], aliases: ['Screening program evaluation and survival artifacts:', 'Lead-Time Bias artificial prolongation of survival time calculated from the moment of diagnosis caused by early detection of disease by screening, without actually changing the natural course of disease or prolonging total lifespan (time of death is unchanged, patient simply lives longer with diagnosis) Creates false illusion that screening improved survival Overcome by measuring Mortality Rate (deaths per population per year) rather than survival time from diagnosis in randomized controlled screening trials', 'Length-Time Bias screening tests preferentially detect slowly progressive indolent long-preclinical-phase tumors with intrinsically better prognosis, while aggressive rapidly growing short-preclinical-phase tumors produce symptoms and present between screening intervals (interval cancers) with worse prognosis Creates false illusion that screening detects curable disease when it actually selectively samples indolent disease Overcome by randomized controlled trials with all-cause and disease-specific mortality endpoints', 'Overdiagnosis screening detects indolent pseudodisease that would never have caused symptoms or death in patient lifetime (e.g. low-grade prostate cancer thyroid microcarcinomas) leading to unnecessary treatments overtreatment morbidity'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_005', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2', name: 'Clinical Trial Phases (Phase 0, I, II, III, IV) & Pharmacovigilance', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PHARM_1_007', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001'], aliases: ['FDA clinical trial phases for investigational new drugs mnemonic SWIM:', 'Phase 0 exploratory human microdosing subtherapeutic doses in 10-15 subjects to assess initial pharmacokinetics and target engagement', 'Phase I (Is it Safe?) evaluates safety toxicity pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics maximum tolerated dose MTD in a SMALL number of HEALTHY human volunteers (20-80 subjects) Exception: toxic chemotherapy agents tested in advanced cancer patients', 'Phase II (Does it Work?) evaluates therapeutic Efficacy dosing requirements optimal dose schedule and adverse effects in a MODERATE number of PATIENTS with the target disease (100-300 subjects)', 'Phase III (Does it Improve over standard?) large multicenter Randomized Controlled Trials comparing new drug to current Standard of Care or placebo in LARGE patient populations (1,000-3,000 subjects) evaluates clinical efficacy safety risk-benefit ratio provides primary evidence for FDA approval and marketing', 'Phase IV (Post-Marketing Surveillance) post-approval open-label observational surveillance of drug in real-world general population detects rare delayed or idiosyncratic adverse drug reactions ADRs and long-term toxicities (e.g. rofecoxib Vioxx cardiovascular risk, troglitazone hepatotoxicity resulting in market withdrawal) voluntary reporting via FDA MedWatch system pharmacovigilance'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_006', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2', name: 'Hierarchy of Evidence, Meta-Analysis, Forest & Funnel Plots', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_1_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_005'], aliases: ['Hierarchy of clinical evidence and quantitative evidence synthesis:', 'Levels of Clinical Evidence: Level 1 Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses of Randomized Controlled Trials RCTs (highest strength of evidence) > Level 2 Individual well-designed Randomized Controlled Trials > Level 3 Prospective Cohort Studies > Level 4 Case-Control Studies > Level 5 Case Series and Case Reports > Level 6 Expert clinical opinion narrative reviews bench research', 'Meta-Analysis statistical pooling and combination of quantitative data from multiple independent clinical trials addressing the same research question increases statistical power and precision of effect estimate', 'Forest Plot graphical presentation of meta-analysis results: Each study represented by a horizontal line (95% Confidence Interval) with a square in the center (point estimate); Size of square is proportional to study Weight (larger sample size = smaller variance = larger weight); Vertical solid line represents Null Effect (RR/OR = 1.0 or Mean Difference = 0); Diamond at bottom represents Pooled Summary Effect Estimate (center of diamond is point estimate, width of diamond is pooled 95% CI; if diamond touches or crosses vertical line of null effect pooled result is not statistically significant)', 'Funnel Plot scatter plot of trial effect size against sample size or standard error used to assess Publication Bias (file-drawer problem: positive studies preferentially published while negative or null trials remain unpublished): Symmetrical inverted funnel indicates absence of publication bias; Asymmetrical plot with missing bottom corner (missing small negative studies) demonstrates Publication Bias'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' }
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3', name: 'Medical Ethics, Legal Principles & Patient Safety', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', legacyId: 'USMLE_11_Ethics', topics: [
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_001', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3', name: 'Core Bioethical Principles: Autonomy, Beneficence, Nonmaleficence & Justice', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_002', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_004'], aliases: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_2_001', 'USMLE_11_2_002', 'Four core pillars of bioethics Beauchamp and Childress:', 'Autonomy respect for patient self-determination right of competent individuals to make decisions regarding their own medical care including the right to accept or REFUSE life-saving treatments (e.g. competent adult Jehovah Witness refusing blood transfusion even if refusal results in death); Requires honest full truthful disclosure Veracity; Paternalism (physician overriding patient autonomous choice for perceived benefit) is unethical in competent patients', 'Beneficence moral duty of physician to act in the best interest of the patient to promote health well-being and prevent harm', 'Nonmaleficence Primum non nocere (First do no harm) obligation to avoid inflicting intentional or unnecessary harm includes balancing therapeutic risk versus benefit; Principle of Double Effect: An action with both a good intended effect (pain relief with opioids) and a foreseen adverse effect (respiratory depression, possible hastening of death) is morally permissible provided the physician primary intent is solely the good effect', 'Justice fair equitable and non-discriminatory distribution of healthcare resources benefits and burdens across society Distributive Justice (e.g. organ allocation based on medical urgency and compatibility not wealth or celebrity status)'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_002', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3', name: 'Informed Consent, Exceptions to Consent (W-ICE) & Minor Assent', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_003'], aliases: ['Informed consent legal and ethical requirement for voluntary authorization before medical interventions:', 'Essential Elements of Informed Consent: 1 Disclosure of diagnosis proposed procedure alternatives foreseeable risks benefits and consequences of refusal 2 Voluntariness free from coercion manipulation 3 Patient Decision-Making Capacity', 'Exceptions to Informed Consent mnemonic W-ICE: 1 Waiver patient explicitly requests not to receive medical details and transfers decision to surrogate 2 Incompetence lack of decision-making capacity 3 Coercion absent / Emergency Implied Consent in life- or limb-threatening situations when patient is incapacitated and surrogate is unavailable 4 Therapeutic Privilege extremely narrow exception when disclosure would cause severe immediate psychological harm', 'Consent in Minors (<18 years): Legal guardian or parent provides informed consent; child provides Assent agreement developmentally appropriate: Parental Refusal: Parents CANNOT refuse life-saving medical care for a minor child (e.g. court order or emergency treatment overrides parental refusal of blood transfusion in minor child of Jehovah Witnesses); Emancipated Minors legal adults: Married, Active duty military, Financially independent / Court-decreed emancipation; Mature Minor Exceptions minors can consent WITHOUT parental knowledge or consent for: Sex (contraception, pregnancy care, STI evaluation/treatment), Drugs (substance abuse treatment), and Mental Health (outpatient psychiatric therapy)'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_003', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3', name: 'Decision-Making Capacity Assessment vs Legal Competency', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 60, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_001', 'USMLE_PSYCH_1_001'], aliases: ['Distinction between clinical capacity and legal competence:', 'Decision-Making Capacity CLINICAL determination made by ANY licensed physician for a SPECIFIC medical decision at a SPECIFIC time: Capacity is decision-specific and dynamic fluctuates (e.g. delirious patient may lack capacity now but regain it tomorrow); Having a psychiatric illness (schizophrenia, bipolar, major depression) does NOT automatically render patient incapacitated if they satisfy criteria for the specific choice', 'Four Essential Elements of Capacity mnemonic CURB: 1 Communicate / express a clear and consistent choice 2 Understand the relevant clinical information diagnosis prognosis and options 3 Reason rationally through treatment risks benefits and logical consequences 4 Appreciate the personal implications of the decision for their own situation', 'Competency LEGAL determination made strictly by a JUDGE in a court of law regarding general legal autonomy (e.g. managing financial estates signing contracts global guardianship)'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'average' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_004', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3', name: 'Advance Directives: Living Wills, Healthcare Proxy & Surrogate Hierarchy', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_006'], aliases: ['Advance directives written legal documents executed when competent directing medical care in the event of future incapacitation:', 'Living Will document specifying which medical interventions the patient desires or refuses (ventilator, artificial nutrition hydration hemodialysis CPR) under specific circumstances (terminal illness, persistent vegetative state)', 'Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (Healthcare Proxy) legal appointment of a designated surrogate decision-maker to make healthcare decisions if patient loses capacity Takes precedence over a living will because the proxy can interpret unanticipated clinical developments in real time', 'Surrogate Hierarchy in absence of designated proxy: Legal Spouse > Adult Children > Parents > Adult Siblings > Other adult relatives; If family members disagree ethics committee consultation or court mediator utilized', 'Ethical Standards for Surrogate Decision-Making: 1 Substituted Judgment Standard PRIMARY standard surrogate must make the decision the PATIENT would have made based on prior stated values wishes conversations stepping into patient shoes 2 Best Interests Standard used ONLY when patient prior wishes are unknown surrogate decides what a reasonable person would determine is in the patient best overall interest'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_005', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3', name: 'Patient Confidentiality, HIPAA & Mandatory Reporting Exceptions', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_001', 'USMLE_PSYCH_4_001'], aliases: ['Confidentiality duty to protect patient privacy Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act HIPAA:', 'Exceptions to Confidentiality Mandatory Reporting requirements mnemonic SAVED:', '1 Suspected Abuse: Child Abuse or neglect MANDATORY reporting to Child Protective Services CPS immediately physician does not need parental consent or proof suspicion alone mandates report; Elder Abuse or vulnerable adult abuse mandatory reporting to Adult Protective Services APS; Intimate Partner Violence domestic violence NOT mandatory to report in most states unless gunshot/stab wound physician must ensure patient immediate safety assess danger and offer domestic violence shelter resources reporting against patient will can endanger patient life', '2 Reportable Infectious Communicable Diseases: Syphilis, Gonorrhea, Chlamydia, HIV, Tuberculosis TB, Measles, Pertussis, foodborne Salmonella must report to local/state department of public health contact tracing partner notification handled by public health authorities', '3 Serious Threat to Third Parties (Tarasoff Duty to Protect / Warn): When a patient makes a credible serious and imminent threat of physical violence against an identifiable individual the physician must warn the intended victim and notify law enforcement', '4 Impaired Drivers: Uncontrolled epilepsy recurrent syncope severe dementia reporting to DMV based on state law', '5 Gunshot and stab wounds mandatory police notification'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_006', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3', name: 'End-of-Life Decisions: DNR Orders, Medical Futility, Palliative Care & Assisted Dying', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_004'], aliases: ['End-of-life care and ethical management of dying:', 'Do Not Resuscitate DNR order specifically prohibits cardiopulmonary resuscitation CPR chest compressions defibrillation intubation in the event of cardiac or respiratory arrest DNR does NOT mean do not treat all other active medical therapies (antibiotics transfusions dialysis pain management ICU care) continue unless separately declined', 'Medical Futility physician is not ethically obligated to provide interventions that have no pathophysiological rationale or cannot achieve the intended clinical goal (e.g. CPR in irreversible multisystem organ failure) Unilateral withdrawal requires ethics consultation second opinion and offering transfer of care to another facility', 'Withholding vs Withdrawing Life-Sustaining Treatment: Ethically and legally IDENTICAL Withdrawing mechanical ventilation or artificial nutrition/hydration at patient or surrogate request is allowing natural disease progression not euthanasia', 'Palliative Care and Hospice: Hospice care for terminally ill patients with estimated prognosis ≤6 months certified by two physicians focuses on symptom control comfort and dignity rather than curative interventions', 'Physician-Assisted Suicide (Medical Aid in Dying) physician prescribes lethal medication that the patient must voluntarily SELF-ADMINISTER (legal in specific jurisdictions); Euthanasia physician directly administers lethal injection (illegal throughout US)'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_007', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3', name: 'Professional Boundaries, Gifts, Romantic Relationships & Impaired Colleagues', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 60, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_001', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_008'], aliases: ['Professionalism and professional boundary management:', 'Gifts from Patients: Modest inexpensive gifts of gratitude (baked goods, inexpensive card) may be accepted with sincere appreciation; Expensive lavish gifts or gifts offered before care in expectation of preferential treatment must be politely declined explaining professional ethics', 'Romantic or Sexual Relationships with Patients: Completely unethical and constitutes gross professional misconduct Sexual relationship with a CURRENT patient is NEVER permitted requires immediate termination of doctor-patient relationship and transfer of care; Sexual contact with FORMER psychiatric patients is permanently unethical and prohibited', 'Impaired Colleague: Physician colleague showing signs of substance use disorder intoxication cognitive impairment or erratic behavior posing danger to patient safety: Primary ethical obligation is PATIENT SAFETY: If imminent patient danger remove colleague immediately from clinical duties; Report to hospital Physician Health Program PHP department chief or state medical licensing board Never ignore or directly confront aggressively', 'Disclosing Medical Errors: Physician has ethical and professional obligation to promptly disclose adverse medical errors to the patient explain what occurred provide prognosis apologize and outline corrective steps prevents malpractice litigation and preserves trust'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'average' },
          { id: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_008', subjectId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH', systemId: 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3', name: 'Patient Safety, Quality Improvement, Swiss Cheese Model & Root Cause Analysis', highYield: true, estimatedStudyMinutes: 75, relatedTopics: ['USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_007', 'USMLE_PUBHEALTH_3_001'], aliases: ['Patient safety science and systems-based error prevention:', 'Active vs Latent Errors: Active Error occurs at frontline operator level (sharp end e.g. nurse administering wrong drug dose, surgeon operating on wrong limb); Latent Error system-level flaw in organizational processes design staffing protocols equipment or culture (blunt end e.g. look-alike drug packaging stored next to each other, understaffed night shift, faulty electronic alert) creates conditions allowing active errors to occur', 'Swiss Cheese Model (James Reason) catastrophic medical errors occur when holes in multiple defensive system layers (pre-op checklist, surgical time-out, bar-code medication scanning, independent pharmacy verification) momentarily align allowing a hazard to reach the patient', 'Root Cause Analysis RCA structured RETROSPECTIVE investigation performed following a Sentinel Event (unexpected patient death, suicide, wrong-site surgery, severe permanent harm) to identify underlying latent SYSTEMIC vulnerabilities rather than assigning individual blame: Uses Fishbone Ishikawa Diagram (people, machines, policies, procedures, environment) and Five Whys methodology', 'Failure Mode and Effects Analysis FMEA PROSPECTIVE systematic analysis performed BEFORE implementing a new process or device to anticipate potential failure modes and prevent harm', 'Handoff Communication Standardization I-PASS tool (Illness severity, Patient summary, Action list, Situation awareness, Synthesis by receiver) reduces preventable medical handoff communication errors'], pyqWeight: 5, difficulty: 'high' }
        ]
      }
